**Mark S. Miller**&nbsp;is an American&nbsp;[computer scientist](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Computer_scientist). He is known for his work as one of the participants in the 1979 hypertext project known as&nbsp;[Project Xanadu](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Project_Xanadu); for inventing&nbsp;[Miller columns](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Miller_columns); and the open-source coordinator of the&nbsp;[E programming language](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/E_(programming_language)). He also designed the&nbsp;[Caja compiler](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Caja_project). Miller is a Senior Research Fellow at the&nbsp;[Foresight Institute](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Foresight_Institute).[[1]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mark_S._Miller#cite_note-1)
Miller earned a BS in&nbsp;[computer science](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Computer_science)&nbsp;from&nbsp;[Yale](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Yale)&nbsp;in 1980 and published his&nbsp;[Johns Hopkins](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Johns_Hopkins_University)&nbsp;PhD thesis in 2006.[[2]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mark_S._Miller#cite_note-2)&nbsp;He is currently Chief Scientist at Agoric[[3]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mark_S._Miller#cite_note-3)&nbsp;and a member of the&nbsp;[ECMAScript](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/ECMAScript)&nbsp;(JavaScript) committee.[[4]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mark_S._Miller#cite_note-4)&nbsp;Previous positions include Chief Architect with the Virus-Safe Computing Initiative at&nbsp;[HP Labs](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/HP_Labs),[[5]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mark_S._Miller#cite_note-5)&nbsp;and research scientist at&nbsp;[Google](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Google)&nbsp;between 2007 and 2017.[[6]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mark_S._Miller#cite_note-6)
Miller's research has focused on language design for secure open systems. At Xerox PARC, he worked on Concurrent Logic Programming systems and Agoric Open Systems. At Sun Labs,[[7]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mark_S._Miller#cite_note-7)&nbsp;(while working for Agorics, an earlier company with a similar name to his current employer) he led the development of WebMart, a framework for buying and selling computing resources (network bandwidth,[[8]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mark_S._Miller#cite_note-8)&nbsp;access to a printer, images, CD jukebox etc.) across the network. At HP Labs he was the architect for the Virus Safe Computing project. While at Google he developed Caja, an environment for secure execution of JavaScript. He has also written articles on&nbsp;[complex adaptive systems](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Complex_adaptive_systems)[[9]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mark_S._Miller#cite_note-9)&nbsp;and risk mitigation strategies for future technologies.[[10]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mark_S._Miller#cite_note-10)
Miller has been pursuing a stated goal of enabling cooperation between untrusting partners.[[11]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mark_S._Miller#cite_note-11)&nbsp;Miller sees this as a fundamental feature required to power economic interactions, and the main piece that has been missing in the toolkit available to software developers. Miller has returned to this issue repeatedly since the Agoric Open Systems Papers from 1988.
Miller's most prominent contributions have been in the area of programming language design, most notably, the E Language, which demonstrated language-based secure distributed computing. The work inspired several adaptations to other programming paradigms. He was also instrumental on the ECMAScript standards committee (TC39) in providing the foundations for development of Secure EcmaScript (SES), a standards track evolution that will make full capability programming available in JavaScript.[[12]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mark_S._Miller#cite_note-12)
Miller's work has been written up in Wired[[13]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mark_S._Miller#cite_note-13)&nbsp;which described his work as the inspiration for database researcher&nbsp;[Michael Stonebraker](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Michael_Stonebraker)'s&nbsp;[Mariposa](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Michael_Stonebraker#Mariposa_and_Cohera), developed at&nbsp;[Berkeley](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/UC_Berkeley).
