ex-col items-end gap-2 text-start break-words whitespace-normal [.text-message+&amp;]:mt-5" data-message-model-slug="gpt-5"><div class="flex w-full flex-col gap-1 empty:hidden first:pt-[3px]"><div class="markdown prose dark:prose-invert w-full break-words dark markdown-new-styling"><p data-start="0" data-end="207">Mark S. Miller is a fascinating figure in the history of programming languages and computer security. Heâ€™s one of those rare people whose work runs like a hidden current beneath todayâ€™s software landscape.</p>
<h3 data-start="209" data-end="230">Key Contributions</h3>
<ul data-start="231" data-end="1634">
<li data-start="231" data-end="407">
<p data-start="233" data-end="407"><strong data-start="233" data-end="259">Project Xanadu (1979):</strong> As a participant in Ted Nelsonâ€™s hypertext dream project, Miller was right there at the messy birth of many ideas that later surfaced in the web.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="408" data-end="575">
<p data-start="410" data-end="575"><strong data-start="410" data-end="429">Miller Columns:</strong> If youâ€™ve ever used Appleâ€™s Finder â€œcolumn viewâ€ or a file browser that unfolds hierarchically left to right, that UI pattern is his invention.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="576" data-end="827">
<p data-start="578" data-end="827"><strong data-start="578" data-end="593">E Language:</strong> Probably his most enduring technical work. E is a capability-based distributed language designed for secure cooperation across mutually suspicious machines. Its ideas later influenced smart contracts and secure distributed systems.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="828" data-end="1136">
<p data-start="830" data-end="1136"><strong data-start="830" data-end="860">Object Capabilities &amp; SES:</strong> Miller has been a central advocate for â€œobject capabilityâ€ security, which strips away ambient authority and forces explicit delegation. He pushed these ideas into mainstream JavaScript through the ECMAScript standards committee, culminating in <strong data-start="1106" data-end="1133">Secure EcmaScript (SES)</strong>.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="1137" data-end="1327">
<p data-start="1139" data-end="1327"><strong data-start="1139" data-end="1158">Caja at Google:</strong> His work on Caja tried to make JavaScript safer to run in untrusted contextsâ€”basically, letting pieces of code cooperate without being able to escape their sandboxes.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="1328" data-end="1634">
<p data-start="1330" data-end="1634"><strong data-start="1330" data-end="1349">Agoric Systems:</strong> Both in the 1980s with K. Eric Drexler and today as Chief Scientist of Agoric, Miller has pursued the vision of using <em data-start="1468" data-end="1487">market mechanisms</em> inside computation. The idea is that programs and resources should negotiate with each other economically, rather than by force of central design.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<h3 data-start="1636" data-end="1659">Intellectual Thread</h3>
<p data-start="1660" data-end="1978">The through-line in all this is a very specific obsession: <strong data-start="1719" data-end="1831">â€œHow do we get strangersâ€”pieces of code, or people mediated through codeâ€”to cooperate safely without trust?â€</strong> His doctoral thesis (<em data-start="1853" data-end="1943">Robust Composition: Towards a Unified Approach to Access Control and Concurrency Control</em>, 2006) formalizes that question.</p>
<h3 data-start="1980" data-end="1993">Influence</h3>
<ul data-start="1994" data-end="2439">
<li data-start="1994" data-end="2131">
<p data-start="1996" data-end="2131">His capability work underpins not just niche languages, but also modern thinking on <strong data-start="2080" data-end="2099">smart contracts</strong> and blockchain architectures.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="2132" data-end="2220">
<p data-start="2134" data-end="2220">Eâ€™s ideas echo in database systems (Stonebraker cited him when developing Mariposa).</p>
</li>
<li data-start="2221" data-end="2295">
<p data-start="2223" data-end="2295">Cajaâ€™s lineage continues in secure JS subsets and todayâ€™s SES tooling.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="2296" data-end="2439">
<p data-start="2298" data-end="2439">The Agoric papers from the late 1980s remain classics, often resurfacing in discussions of decentralized computing and resource allocation.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<h3 data-start="2441" data-end="2455">Fun Detail</h3>
<p data-start="2456" data-end="2802">Despite being a deeply technical thinker, heâ€™s also a bridge figure: working at Xerox PARC, Sun Labs, HP Labs, Google, and now a startup, while simultaneously orbiting communities like the Foresight Institute. His career is almost a living map of how 1970sâ€“80s computer utopianism got woven into the practical security problems of the modern web.</p>
<hr data-start="2804" data-end="2807">
<p data-start="2809" data-end="3040" data-is-last-node="" data-is-only-node="">Would you like me to give you a <strong data-start="2841" data-end="2869">timeline-style breakdown</strong> of his career (with projects in order), or a <strong data-start="2915" data-end="2937">thematic breakdown</strong> (grouping hypertext, UI, capabilities, distributed markets) so you can see how the threads cross over?</p></div></div></div></div><div class="flex min-h-[46px] justify-start"><div class="touch:-me-2 touch:-ms-3.5 -ms-2.5 -me-1 flex flex-wrap items-center gap-y-4 p-1 select-none touch:w-[calc(100%+--spacing(3.5))] -mt-1 w-[calc(100%+--spacing(2.5))] duration-[1.5s] focus-within:transition-none hover:transition-none pointer-events-none [mask-image:linear-gradient(to_right,black_33%,transparent_66%)] [mask-size:300%_100%] [mask-position:100%_0%] motion-safe:transition-[mask-position] group-hover/turn-messages:pointer-events-auto group-hover/turn-messages:[mask-position:0_0] group-focus-within/turn-messages:pointer-events-auto group-focus-within/turn-messages:[mask-position:0_0] has-data-[state=open]:pointer-events-auto has-data-[state=open]:[mask-position:0_0]" style="mask-position: 0% 0%;"><button class="text-token-text-secondary hover:bg-token-bg-secondary rounded-lg" aria-label="Copy" aria-pressed="false" data-testid="copy-turn-action-button" data-state="closed"><span class="flex items-center justify-center touch:w-10 h-8 w-8"><svg width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 20 20" fill="currentColor" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" class="icon"><path d="M12.668 10.667C12.668 9.95614 12.668 9.46258 12.6367 9.0791C12.6137 8.79732 12.5758 8.60761 12.5244 8.46387L12.4688 8.33399C12.3148 8.03193 12.0803 7.77885 11.793 7.60254L11.666 7.53125C11.508 7.45087 11.2963 7.39395 10.9209 7.36328C10.5374 7.33197 10.0439 7.33203 9.33301 7.33203H6.5C5.78896 7.33203 5.29563 7.33195 4.91211 7.36328C4.63016 7.38632 4.44065 7.42413 4.29688 7.47559L4.16699 7.53125C3.86488 7.68518 3.61186 7.9196 