-col gap-1 empty:hidden first:pt-[3px]"><div class="markdown prose dark:prose-invert w-full break-words dark markdown-new-styling"><p data-start="0" data-end="433">I wasnâ€™t able to find any direct quotes from Mark S. Miller in which he explicitly describes Big Tech as â€œa bunch of interlocking arms races.â€ That characterization may reflect an interpretation of his broader philosophical viewsâ€”about competitive escalation within technology ecosystems, especially around security, market dominance, and technical capabilityâ€”but I couldnâ€™t verify a specific statement that uses that exact language.</p>
<p data-start="435" data-end="635">However, the metaphor of "arms races" is widely used today to describe competitive dynamics in the tech industryâ€”especially in areas like AI, cloud infrastructure, and platform dominance. For example:</p>
<ul data-start="637" data-end="1107">
<li data-start="637" data-end="849">
<p data-start="639" data-end="849">The term â€œAI arms raceâ€ is commonly employed to describe the escalating competition between countries, notably the U.S. and China, vying for leadership in AI development <span class="" data-state="closed"><span class="ms-1 inline-flex max-w-full items-center relative top-[-0.094rem] animate-[show_150ms_ease-in]" data-testid="webpage-citation-pill"><a href="https://papers.ssrn.com/sol3/Delivery.cfm/5120918.pdf?abstractid=5120918&amp;mirid=1&amp;utm_source=chatgpt.com" target="_blank" rel="noopener" alt="https://papers.ssrn.com/sol3/Delivery.cfm/5120918.pdf?abstractid=5120918&amp;mirid=1&amp;utm_source=chatgpt.com" class="flex h-4.5 overflow-hidden rounded-xl px-2 text-[9px] font-medium text-token-text-secondary! bg-[#F4F4F4]! dark:bg-[#303030]! transition-colors duration-150 ease-in-out"><span class="relative start-0 bottom-0 flex h-full w-full items-center"><span class="flex h-4 w-full items-center justify-between overflow-hidden" style="opacity: 1; transform: none;"><span class="max-w-full grow truncate overflow-hidden text-center">SSRN</span></span></span></a></span></span>.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="850" data-end="1107">
<p data-start="852" data-end="1107">More broadly, discussions about Big Tech often refer to these firms as engaging in platform rivalries reminiscent of arms races, particularly when it touches on advanced hardware, computational power, or market reach <span class="" data-state="closed"><span class="ms-1 inline-flex max-w-full items-center relative top-[-0.094rem] animate-[show_150ms_ease-in]" data-testid="webpage-citation-pill"><a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artificial_intelligence_arms_race?utm_source=chatgpt.com" target="_blank" rel="noopener" alt="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artificial_intelligence_arms_race?utm_source=chatgpt.com" class="flex h-4.5 overflow-hidden rounded-xl px-2 text-[9px] font-medium text-token-text-secondary! bg-[#F4F4F4]! dark:bg-[#303030]! transition-colors duration-150 ease-in-out"><span class="relative start-0 bottom-0 flex h-full w-full items-center"><span class="flex h-4 w-full items-center justify-between" style="opacity: 1; transform: none;"><span class="max-w-full grow truncate overflow-hidden text-center">Wikipedia</span><span class="-me-1 flex h-full items-center rounded-full px-1 text-[#8F8F8F]">+1</span></span></span></a></span></span>.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<p data-start="1109" data-end="1259">If that summary aligns with how you understood Millerâ€™s perspective, it makes senseâ€”but as of now, I canâ€™t attribute that phraseology directly to him.</p>
