rem))] origin-top-left flex-col gap-2 overflow-x-clip" style="opacity: 1; transform: none;"><span class="" data-state="closed"><div class="relative w-full text-start"><div class="flex w-full items-start justify-between text-start flex-col"><button class="flex w-full items-center gap-0.5"><span class=""><span class="flex items-center gap-1 text-start align-middle truncate text-token-text-secondary hover:text-token-text-primary" style="opacity: 1;">Thought for 16s</span></span></button></div></div></span></div></div><div data-message-author-role="assistant" data-message-id="5cef2720-0489-4c9b-b23f-31eebd7ade9b" dir="auto" class="min-h-8 text-message relative flex w-full flex-col items-end gap-2 text-start break-words whitespace-normal [.text-message+&amp;]:mt-5" data-message-model-slug="gpt-5-thinking"><div class="flex w-full flex-col gap-1 empty:hidden first:pt-[3px]"><div class="markdown prose dark:prose-invert w-full break-words dark markdown-new-styling"><p data-start="0" data-end="335">Youâ€™re pointing at the failure mode that sits <em data-start="46" data-end="56">upstream</em> of both menâ€™s strategies: <strong data-start="83" data-end="113">intersubjectivity collapse</strong>â€”the loss of shared public facts, norms, and legitimacy. Once common knowledge fractures, <em data-start="203" data-end="208">any</em> technical plan (pause or secure-by-design) loses traction, and the path to civil or interstate conflict shortens dramatically.</p>
<p data-start="337" data-end="420">Below is a compact addendum you can bolt onto your study guide to center this risk.</p>
<h1 data-start="422" data-end="468">Intersubjectivity Collapse: The Missing Crux</h1>
<h2 data-start="470" data-end="483">Definition</h2>
<p data-start="484" data-end="742">A systemic breakdown of <strong data-start="508" data-end="538">shared epistemic standards</strong> (what counts as evidence), <strong data-start="566" data-end="583">trust anchors</strong> (institutions, procedures, credentials), and <strong data-start="629" data-end="658">coordination focal points</strong> (laws, elections, markets), producing durable factions with incompatible realities.</p>
<h2 data-start="744" data-end="779">Why it undercuts both strategies</h2>
<ul data-start="780" data-end="997">
<li data-start="780" data-end="879">
<p data-start="782" data-end="879"><strong data-start="782" data-end="815">Yudkowskyâ€™s pause/enforcement</strong> needs broad compliance and verification; collapse removes both.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="880" data-end="997">
<p data-start="882" data-end="997"><strong data-start="882" data-end="913">Millerâ€™s cooperative fabric</strong> presumes credible commitment and adjudication; collapse dissolves those substrates.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<h2 data-start="999" data-end="1024">Drivers (AI-amplified)</h2>
<ol data-start="1025" data-end="1640">
<li data-start="1025" data-end="1132">
<p data-start="1028" data-end="1132"><strong data-start="1028" data-end="1047">Attribution fog</strong>: deniable bio/cyber actions; rapid synthetic deception; indistinguishable forgeries.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="1133" data-end="1227">
<p data-start="1136" data-end="1227"><strong data-start="1136" data-end="1167">Adversarial personalization</strong>: micro-targeted narratives that self-seal against rebuttal.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="1228" data-end="1315">
<p data-start="1231" data-end="1315"><strong data-start="1231" data-end="1260">Institutional brittleness</strong>: slow, opaque, or partisan responses erode legitimacy.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="1316" data-end="1432">
<p data-start="1319" data-end="1432"><strong data-start="1319" data-end="1346">Economic shock coupling</strong>: layoffs, price spikes, or outages blamed on â€œthe other side,â€ compounding grievance.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="1433" data-end="1526">
<p data-start="1436" data-end="1526"><strong data-start="1436" data-end="1457">Platform fracture</strong>: incompatible moderation/provenance regimes across media ecosystems.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="1527" data-end="1640">
<p data-start="1530" data-end="1640"><strong data-start="1530" data-end="1560">Ambiguous metrics of truth</strong>: â€œmodel says soâ€ replaces auditable method; expert authority becomes factional.</p>
</li>
</ol>
<h2 data-start="1642" data-end="1694">Escalation pathways to civil/ interstate conflict</h2>
<ul data-start="1695" data-end="1972">
<li data-start="1695" data-end="1781">
<p data-start="1697" data-end="1781"><strong data-start="1697" data-end="1721">Misattributed strike</strong> (bio/cyber/infra) â†’ retaliatory spiral under time pressure.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="1782" data-end="1879">
<p data-start="1784" data-end="1879"><strong data-start="1784" data-end="1809">Contested sovereignty</strong> (elections/courts) â†’ parallel institutions and security mobilization.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="1880" data-end="1972">
<p data-start="1882" data-end="1972"><strong data-start="1882" data-end="1907">Information embargoes</strong> between blocs â†’ real supply-chain decoupling and force postures.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<h2 data-start="1974" data-end="2027">Early-warning indicators (operational, measurable)</h2>
<ul data-start="2028" data-end="2542">
<li data-start="2028" data-end="2144">
<p data-start="2030" data-end="2144"><strong data-start="2030" data-end="2057">Cross-camp fact overlap</strong> drops below a threshold (e.g., &lt;30% agreement on a basket of verifiable propositions).</p>
</li>
<li data-start="2145" data-end="2234">
<p data-start="2147" data-end="2234"><strong data-start="2147" data-end="2166">Rumor half-life</strong> shortens (viral falsehoods decay slower than verified corrections).</p>
</li>
<li data-start="2235" data-end="2339">
<p data-start="2237" data-end="2339"><strong data-start="2237" data-end="2260">Attribution latency</strong> lengthens (time to confident incident attribution rises quarter-over-quarter).</p>
</li>
<li data-start="2340" data-end="2430">
<p data-start="2342" data-end="2430"><strong data-start="2342" data-end="2376">Bot/human conversational ratio</strong> and inauthentic coordination spikes in civic domains.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="2431" data-end="2542">
<p data-start="2433" data-end="2542"><strong data-start="2433" data-end="2456">Provenance coverage</strong> stagnates (low fraction of high-reach media with cryptographic origin/ edit history).</p>
</li>
</ul>
<h2 data-start="2544" data-end="2598">Mitigation architecture (actionable, not hand-wavy)</h2>
<p data-start="2600" data-end="2642"><strong data-start="2600" data-end="2642">A. Authenticity &amp; accountability stack</strong></p>
<ul data-start="2643" data-end="3048">
<li data-start="2643" data-end="2791">
<p data-start="2645" data-end="2791"><strong data-start="2645" data-end="2667">Default provenance</strong> (content credentials across major cameras, editors, platforms); machine-verifiable edit trails; human-readable disclosures.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="2792" data-end="2920">
<p data-start="2794" data-end="2920"><strong data-start="2794" data-end="2818">Bounded watermarking</strong> for model outputs plus <em data-start="2842" data-end="2862">provenance primacy</em> (treat watermarks as a hint, provenance as the standard).</p>
</li>
<li data-start="2921" data-end="3048">
<p data-start="2923" data-end="3048"><strong data-start="2923" data-end="2972">Verifiable identity for <em data-start="2949" data-end="2961">high-reach</em> accounts</strong> (selective disclosure/zk-proofs for role or jurisdiction; no universal ID).</p>
</li>
</ul>
<p data-start="3050" data-end="3091"><strong data-start="3050" data-end="3091">B. Epistemic institutions &amp; protocols</strong></p>
<ul data-start="3092" data-end="3503">
<li data-start="3092" data-end="3238">
<p data-start="3094" data-end="3238"><strong data-start="3094" data-end="3134">Rapid, independent attribution cells</strong> (bio/cyber/infra) with red-team adversarial review; publish graded confidence with uncertainty budgets.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="3239" data-end="3384">
<p data-start="3241" data-end="3384"><strong data-start="3241" data-end="3262">Civic model evals</strong>: standardized tests for persuasion/manipulation competence; sandbox constraints for agentic systems in political domains.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="3385" data-end="3503">
<p data-start="3387" data-end="3503"><strong data-start="3387" data-end="3420">Structured public retractions</strong>: mandated correction channels with equal or greater reach than the original error.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<p data-start="3505" data-end="3540"><strong data-start="3505" data-end="3540">C. Market &amp; coordination levers</strong></p>
<ul data-start="3541" data-end="3822">
<li data-start="3541" data-end="3681">
<p data-start="3543" data-end="3681"><strong data-start="3543" data-end="3576">Insured prediction mechanisms</strong> on adjudicable claims (with identity-staked participation) to re-establish public odds as a focal point.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="3682" data-end="3822">
<p data-start="3684" data-end="3822"><strong data-start="3684" data-end="3707">Crisis â€œslow-lanesâ€</strong>: rate-limited virality for unverifiable, high-impact claims during defined emergencies; audited after-action logs.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<p data-start="3824" data-end="3872"><strong data-start="3824" data-end="3872">D. Secure compute substrate (Miller-aligned)</strong></p>
<ul data-start="3873" data-end="4099">
<li data-start="3873" data-end="3963">
<p data-start="3875" data-end="3963"><strong data-start="3875" data-end="3908">Object-capability containment</strong> for agentic services; least-authority by construction.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="3964" data-end="4099">
<p data-start="3966" data-end="4099"><strong data-start="3966" data-end="4008">Memory-safe, formally verified kernels</strong> at choke points; hardware telemetry for anomaly detection (with civil-liberty guardrails).</p>
</li>
</ul>
<p data-start="4101" data-end="4129"><strong data-start="4101" data-end="4129">E. Governance guardrails</strong></p>
<ul data-start="4130" data-end="4311">
<li data-start="4130" data-end="4224">
<p data-start="4132" data-end="4224"><strong data-start="4132" data-end="4172">Attribution-before-retaliation norms</strong> with shared technical playbooks across rival blocs.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="4225" data-end="4311">
<p data-start="4227" data-end="4311"><strong data-start="4227" data-end="4257">Sunsetted emergency powers</strong> tied to measurable attribution/provenance milestones.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<h2 data-start="4313" data-end="4361">What to ask of foundation-model providers now</h2>
<ul data-start="4362" data-end="4749">
<li data-start="4362" data-end="4422">
<p data-start="4364" data-end="4422">Ship <strong data-start="4369" data-end="4397">provenance on by default</strong> for all generated media.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="4423" data-end="4546">
<p data-start="4425" data-end="4546">Enforce <strong data-start="4433" data-end="4465">political-domain constraints</strong> for autonomous agents (no targeted outreach, fundraising, or voter-file access).</p>
</li>
<li data-start="4547" data-end="4665">
<p data-start="4549" data-end="4665">Publish <strong data-start="4557" data-end="4597">persuasion and deception eval scores</strong>; commit to caps or mitigations where capabilities exceed red lines.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="4666" data-end="4749">
<p data-start="4668" data-end="4749">Offer <strong data-start="4674" data-end="4699">forensic support APIs</strong> to accredited investigators to speed attribution.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<h2 data-start="4751" data-end="4787">Research agenda (tight, testable)</h2>
<ol data-start="4788" data-end="5267">
<li data-start="4788" data-end="4899">
<p data-start="4791" data-end="4899"><strong data-start="4791" data-end="4819">Common-knowledge indices</strong>: composite measures of cross-camp fact overlap and trust anchors; track weekly.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="4900" data-end="5019">
<p data-start="4903" data-end="5019"><strong data-start="4903" data-end="4922">Attribution TTR</strong>: reduce time-to-reasoned-attribution with pre-negotiated evidence standards and shared datasets.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="5020" data-end="5165">
<p data-start="5023" data-end="5165"><strong data-start="5023" data-end="5048">Intervention AB-tests</strong>: evaluate rumor slow-lanes, provenance salience UI, and identity-staked discourse for downstream violence reduction.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="5166" data-end="5267">
<p data-start="5169" data-end="5267"><strong data-start="5169" data-end="5203">Agentic containment benchmarks</strong>: OCAP sandboxes vs. discretionary ACLs under red-team pressure.</p>
</li>
</ol>
<h2 data-start="5269" data-end="5315">How this reframes the Yudkowskyâ€“Miller crux</h2>
<ul data-start="5316" data-end="5645">
<li data-start="5316" data-end="5645">
<p data-start="5318" data-end="5440"><strong data-start="5318" data-end="5351">New Crux 0 (prior to theirs):</strong> Can we <em data-start="5359" data-end="5396">maintain intersubjective legitimacy</em> long enough to implement either strategy?</p>
<ul data-start="5443" data-end="5645">
<li data-start="5443" data-end="5522">
<p data-start="5445" data-end="5522">If <strong data-start="5448" data-end="5454">no</strong>, a pause canâ€™t be enforced and a cooperative fabric canâ€™t cohere.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="5525" data-end="5645">
<p data-start="5527" data-end="5645">If <strong data-start="5530" data-end="5537">yes</strong>, the feasibility of both increasesâ€”enforcement for a pause, and verifiable commitments for Millerâ€™s fabric.</p>
</li>
</ul>
</li>
</ul>
<p data-start="5647" data-end="5801">If you want, I can fold this into your guide as a new section (â€œCrux 0: Intersubjectivity Collapseâ€) plus a one-page checklist for policymakers/engineers.</p>
