 data-start="0" data-end="83">Hereâ€™s how we can slot your point into the study guide as a dedicated subsection:</p>
<hr data-start="85" data-end="88">
<h2 data-start="90" data-end="162">Invisible Exclusions: Cognitive Minorities in the Attention Economy</h2>
<p data-start="164" data-end="380">Modern civilization doesnâ€™t only marginalize plants, animals, women, or children; it also marginalizes <strong data-start="267" data-end="291">cognitive minorities</strong> â€” people whose inner perceptual and imaginative styles diverge from the cultural norm.</p>
<ul data-start="382" data-end="1185">
<li data-start="382" data-end="651">
<p data-start="384" data-end="651"><strong data-start="384" data-end="398">Aphantasia</strong> (lack of mental imagery) and <strong data-start="428" data-end="444">anendophasia</strong> (lack of inner monologue) are not usually framed as disabilities. Yet in an economy that rewards <strong data-start="542" data-end="602">constant self-promotion, visualization, and storytelling</strong>, they place people at a systemic disadvantage.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="652" data-end="951">
<p data-start="654" data-end="951">The <strong data-start="658" data-end="679">attention economy</strong> assumes every mind is equally capable of projecting compelling narratives about the self. When those narratives become the ticket to employment, status, or belonging, individuals without the culturally privileged forms of imagination and narration are quietly excluded.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="952" data-end="1185">
<p data-start="954" data-end="1185">This is an <strong data-start="965" data-end="997">intersubjectivity fault line</strong>: as society increasingly equates worth with performance in digital arenas, those whose cognition doesnâ€™t translate smoothly into attention-seeking formats risk being rendered invisible.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<p data-start="1187" data-end="1584">Just as <strong data-start="1195" data-end="1224">roads fragment ecosystems</strong> and <strong data-start="1229" data-end="1255">feeds fragment meaning</strong>, the attention economy fragments humanity by creating <strong data-start="1310" data-end="1330">cognitive castes</strong>. Those who can continuously narrate and promote thrive; those who canâ€™t are marginalized. This is another vector by which intersubjectivity collapse advances: shared meaning narrows to the language of promotion, leaving out entire ways of being human.</p>
