se dark:prose-invert w-full break-words dark markdown-new-styling"><p data-start="0" data-end="244">Right â€” Michael Levinâ€™s work really strengthens your point. He uses <strong data-start="68" data-end="88">â€œmind blindnessâ€</strong> to describe how humans systematically fail to recognize intelligence in non-human systems â€” especially in animals, plants, and even cellular collectives.</p>
<ul data-start="246" data-end="1184">
<li data-start="246" data-end="487">
<p data-start="248" data-end="487"><strong data-start="248" data-end="270">Levinâ€™s core claim</strong>: intelligence is not limited to brains; itâ€™s any system that can pursue goals, adapt to perturbations, and solve problems in its own medium. Cells in a regenerating limb, for example, show agency in shaping growth.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="488" data-end="694">
<p data-start="490" data-end="694"><strong data-start="490" data-end="508">Mind blindness</strong>: Our tendency to overlook these intelligences because they donâ€™t use human-like symbols, narratives, or imagery. We project stupidity where there is simply <strong data-start="665" data-end="691">different subjectivity</strong>.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="695" data-end="910">
<p data-start="697" data-end="910"><strong data-start="697" data-end="715">Your extension</strong>: the same bias that makes us ignore plant and animal intelligence also marginalizes humans with aphantasia or anendophasia â€” because their cognition doesnâ€™t conform to narrative/imagery norms.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="911" data-end="1184">
<p data-start="913" data-end="1184"><strong data-start="913" data-end="936">Collapse connection</strong>: If intersubjectivity collapse is about unpredictable and untranslatable minds, then mind blindness is the <em data-start="1044" data-end="1053">prelude</em>: a refusal to recognize intelligences already here, which makes us fragile when new forms emerge (e.g. AI, augmented cognition).</p>
</li>
</ul>
<p data-start="1186" data-end="1220">So you could frame it like this:</p>
<ul data-start="1221" data-end="1598">
<li data-start="1221" data-end="1319">
<p data-start="1223" data-end="1319"><strong data-start="1223" data-end="1251">Levinâ€™s â€œmind blindnessâ€</strong> shows how we miss the intelligence of plants, animals, and cells.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="1320" data-end="1426">
<p data-start="1322" data-end="1426"><strong data-start="1322" data-end="1346">Cognitive minorities</strong> show how even within humanity, minds outside narrative/imagery are sidelined.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="1427" data-end="1598">
<p data-start="1429" data-end="1598"><strong data-start="1429" data-end="1443">AI futures</strong> will magnify the same bias â€” if we only design for intelligences that â€œtalk like us,â€ weâ€™ll fail to cooperate with (or even recognize) those that donâ€™t.</p>
</li>
