-invert w-full break-words dark markdown-new-styling"><p data-start="0" data-end="401">Thatâ€™s a fascinating angle. The <strong data-start="32" data-end="50">assembly index</strong> (as used in origin-of-life research and complexity science) measures the minimum number of steps required to build a given structure from its basic components. Itâ€™s meant to distinguish things that arise easily from physics/chemistry (low assembly index) versus things that suggest directed processes (high assembly index, e.g. proteins, machines).</p>
<p data-start="403" data-end="463">But there may indeed be <strong data-start="427" data-end="440">loopholes</strong> in how itâ€™s applied:</p>
<ul data-start="465" data-end="1588">
<li data-start="465" data-end="720">
<p data-start="467" data-end="720"><strong data-start="467" data-end="493">Reuse of substructures</strong>: If a system has access to pre-assembled modules (e.g. repeating motifs in polymers, or standardized software libraries), the effective assembly index collapses. Complexity can be bootstrapped without â€œpaying the full cost.â€</p>
</li>
<li data-start="721" data-end="906">
<p data-start="723" data-end="906"><strong data-start="723" data-end="738">Parallelism</strong>: The index assumes a linear build sequence, but many natural processes are massively parallel. Parallel pathways can drastically shorten the real â€œtime to assembly.â€</p>
</li>
<li data-start="907" data-end="1142">
<p data-start="909" data-end="1142"><strong data-start="909" data-end="931">Context dependence</strong>: Some structures look astronomically unlikely in isolation, but in a rich environment (e.g. Earthâ€™s oceans, or the digital commons) precursors are already abundant. This reduces effective assembly difficulty.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="1143" data-end="1380">
<p data-start="1145" data-end="1380"><strong data-start="1145" data-end="1159">Degeneracy</strong>: Many distinct structures can fulfill the same function. The assembly index of one particular molecule might be huge, but life doesnâ€™t need <em data-start="1300" data-end="1316">that exact one</em> â€” just any of a vast family. So probability mass spreads out.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="1381" data-end="1588">
<p data-start="1383" data-end="1588"><strong data-start="1383" data-end="1406">Information leakage</strong>: If external agents (humans, AI, evolutionary processes) leave behind â€œscaffolds,â€ then future builders inherit shortcuts. The index doesnâ€™t always account for cumulative culture.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<p data-start="1590" data-end="2042">This is where it connects back to your concerns: <strong data-start="1639" data-end="1744">intersubjectivity collapse, attention economies, and AGI arms races all exploit loopholes in assembly</strong>. Instead of painstakingly building mutual understanding, platforms shortcut through algorithmic nudges. Instead of diverse emergent intelligences, AI companies reuse training scaffolds and modules. Complexity that looks â€œhardâ€ in principle becomes trivial once scaffolding and reuse are allowed.</p>
<p data-start="2044" data-end="2272" data-is-last-node="" data-is-only-node="">Do you want me to sketch how this loophole logic could be formalized â€” e.g. as a modified <strong data-start="2134" data-end="2164">â€œeffective assembly indexâ€</strong> that discounts for reuse, parallelism, and degeneracy â€” so it can slot into your larger theoretical system?</p></div></div></div></div><div class="flex min-h-[46px] justify-start"><div class="touch:-me-2 touch:-ms-3.5 -ms-2.5 -me-1 flex flex-wrap items-center gap-y-4 p-1 select-none touch:w-[calc(100%+--spacing(3.5))] -mt-1 w-[calc(100%+--spacing(2.5))] duration-[1.5s] focus-within:transition-none hover:transition-none pointer-events-none [mask-image:linear-gradient(to_right,black_33%,transparent_66%)] [mask-size:300%_100%] [mask-position:100%_0%] motion-safe:transition-[mask-position] group-hover/turn-messages:pointer-events-auto group-hover/turn-messages:[mask-position:0_0] group-focus-within/turn-messages:pointer-events-auto group-focus-within/turn-messages:[mask-position:0_0] has-data-[state=open]:pointer-events-auto has-data-[state=open]:[mask-position:0_0]" style="mask-position: 0% 0%;"><button class="text-token-text-secondary hover:bg-token-bg-secondary rounded-lg" aria-label="Copy" aria-pressed="false" data-testid="copy-turn-action-button" data-state="closed"><span class="flex items-center justify-center touch:w-10 h-8 w-8"><svg width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 20 20" fill="currentColor" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" class="icon"><path d="M12.668 10.667C12.668 9.95614 12.668 9.46258 12.6367 9.0791C12.6137 8.79732 12.5758 8.60761 12.5244 8.46387L12.4688 8.33399C12.3148 8.03193 12.0803 7.77885 11.793 7.60254L11.666 7.53125C11.508 7.45087 11.2963 7.39395 10.9209 7.36328C10.5374 7.33197 10.0439 7.33203 9.33301 7.33203H6.5C5.78896 7.33203 5.29563 7.33195 4.91211 7.36328C4.63016 7.38632 4.44065 7.42413 4.29688 7.47559L4.16699 7.53125C3.86488 7.68518 3.61186 7.9196 3.43555 8.20703L3.36524 8.33399C3.28478 8.