<hr data-start="147" data-end="150">
<h3 data-start="152" data-end="172">Integration Path</h3>
<p data-start="174" data-end="217"><strong data-start="174" data-end="215">1. Bridging from Yudkowsky vs. Miller</strong></p>
<ul data-start="218" data-end="846">
<li data-start="218" data-end="357">
<p data-start="220" data-end="357">Yudkowsky stresses that unaligned ASI will coordinate at speeds and depths inaccessible to humans, treating us as irrelevant â€œstatues.â€</p>
</li>
<li data-start="358" data-end="494">
<p data-start="360" data-end="494">Miller emphasizes the possibility of decentralized frameworks and institutional innovations that might preserve human participation.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="495" data-end="846">
<p data-start="497" data-end="846"><strong data-start="497" data-end="551">Intersubjectivity collapse reframes both positions</strong>: even before ASI reaches â€œkill-allâ€ thresholds, the breakdown of shared subjectivity between human and non-human minds could destabilize civilization. Neither pure shutdown (Yudkowsky) nor institutional innovation (Miller) is safe if <em data-start="786" data-end="843">agents cannot even recognize each other as intelligible</em>.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<p data-start="848" data-end="887"><strong data-start="848" data-end="885">2. Attention Economy as Precursor</strong></p>
<ul data-start="888" data-end="1400">
<li data-start="888" data-end="1007">
<p data-start="890" data-end="1007">Todayâ€™s big tech platforms (selling compute, storage, and attention) already foreshadow intersubjectivity collapse.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="1008" data-end="1196">
<p data-start="1010" data-end="1196">They privilege people who can narrate, visualize, and self-promote effectively, while marginalizing those with <strong data-start="1121" data-end="1135">aphantasia</strong> (no mental imagery) or <strong data-start="1159" data-end="1175">anendophasia</strong> (no inner speech).</p>
</li>
<li data-start="1197" data-end="1400">
<p data-start="1199" data-end="1400">This is a microcosm of the larger collapse: when communication infrastructures systematically exclude certain minds, trust erodes and whole populations are rendered unintelligible within the economy.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<p data-start="1402" data-end="1448"><strong data-start="1402" data-end="1446">3. Religious Narratives as Parallel Case</strong></p>
<ul data-start="1449" data-end="1928">
<li data-start="1449" data-end="1621">
<p data-start="1451" data-end="1621">Christianity, Islam, and atheism exemplify mutually incompatible narrative systems. Each encodes a complete worldview with its own â€œinternal logicâ€ and moral authority.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="1622" data-end="1736">
<p data-start="1624" data-end="1736">When these collide without shared intersubjective ground, they generate mistrust, conflict, and sometimes war.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="1737" data-end="1928">
<p data-start="1739" data-end="1928">The same dynamic scales up in intersubjectivity collapse: instead of a few competing religions, we may face thousands of divergent cognitive architectures, each with its own value system.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<p data-start="1930" data-end="1969"><strong data-start="1930" data-end="1967">4. Collapse Dynamics and Conflict</strong></p>
<ul data-start="1970" data-end="2487">
<li data-start="1970" data-end="2083">
<p data-start="1972" data-end="2083">Intersubjectivity collapse predicts <strong data-start="2008" data-end="2037">civil wars and world wars</strong> as soon as trust between minds breaks down.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="2084" data-end="2328">
<p data-start="2086" data-end="2328">Yudkowskyâ€™s fear of ASI slaughter and Millerâ€™s hope for cooperative coalitions both presuppose some common interpretive framework. But <strong data-start="2221" data-end="2293">if agents literally cannot model each otherâ€™s motivations or signals</strong>, even minimal cooperation fails.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="2329" data-end="2487">
<p data-start="2331" data-end="2487">The â€œarms raceâ€ frame that Miller invoked (bio, cyber, AI) thus extends: not only offensive vs. defensive tech, but intelligible vs. unintelligible minds.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<p data-start="2489" data-end="2515"><strong data-start="2489" data-end="2513">5. Toward Mitigation</strong></p>
<ul data-start="2516" data-end="3157">
<li data-start="2516" data-end="2795">
<p data-start="2518" data-end="2795">The <strong data-start="2522" data-end="2579">Effective Assembly Index for Mind Recognition (EAIMR)</strong> youâ€™ve been developing offers a way to extend recognition beyond narrators and self-promoters. It allows termite mounds, forests, atypical humans, or non-narrative AIs to be recognized as minds, not ignored noise.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="2796" data-end="2950">
<p data-start="2798" data-end="2950">Future-proofed laws and institutions should be <strong data-start="2845" data-end="2888">species-agnostic and cognition-agnostic</strong>, avoiding reliance on proxies like age, imagery, or speech.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="2951" data-end="3157">
<p data-start="2953" data-end="3157">This aligns with Millerâ€™s instinct for institutional innovation but demands more radical foundations: instead of just smart contracts, <strong data-start="3088" data-end="3116">mind-recognition metrics</strong> must underwrite trust and cooperation.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<hr data-start="3159" data-end="3162">
<h3 data-start="3164" data-end="3188">References to Anchor</h3>
<ul data-start="3189" data-end="3789">
<li data-start="3189" data-end="3322">
<p data-start="3191" data-end="3322"><strong data-start="3191" data-end="3204">Yudkowsky</strong>: certainty of ASI as â€œasteroid impactâ€ threat; orthogonality thesis; the exclusion of humans from ASI coordination.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="3323" data-end="3475">
<p data-start="3325" data-end="3475"><strong data-start="3325" data-end="3335">Miller</strong>: emergent intelligence of institutions; â€œJames Madison 2.0â€; arms races amplified by compute; smart contracts as coordination mechanisms.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="3476" data-end="3642">
<p data-start="3478" data-end="3642"><strong data-start="3478" data-end="3499">Pawel Pachniewski</strong> (Intersubjectivity Collapse): collapse as great filter candidate; orthogonality of behavior and subjectivity; fragmentation of civilization.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="3643" data-end="3789">
<p data-start="3645" data-end="3789"><strong data-start="3645" data-end="3676">Attention Economy Critiques</strong>: platforms optimizing for engagement, privileging narrative/imagistic minds, marginalizing atypical cognition.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<hr data-start="3791" data-end="3794">
